lisa-design:
- Rating happens in Scanner.Scan (after the runtime check), so `check`, the scan's summary and the planner all see the same severities; nothing downstream re-rates.
- Alias order: BIT- aliases first (max among them); only if none has a vector, every other alias (CVE-, GHSA-, RUSTSEC-...). The finding's own IDs are never re-fetched (the osv-scanner report carries them). ScoreFrom records the alias used.
- The cache lives on imagescan.OSV (one per run). 404 = "no record" (cached, no warning). Errors are not cached; after 3 consecutive failures the run stops asking (circuit breaker), bounding an outage to ~3 client timeouts.
- Lookup failures never fail the scan: Scan.Warnings -> check: ::warning:: + summary callout; scan: repo note "**warning**: <ref>: ...", deduped per repo.
- Planner excludes Go stdlib (Ecosystem Go, Source stdlib) from rules 1-3 and notes them once per image: without it rule 1 bumps golang/Go-tool base images to their next patch for what is a Go rebuild (proven by mutating the test), and an un-rebuilt upstream would block OS-fix fast-tracks. Go *module* findings stay in the plan (unchanged behaviour). Stdlib still counts in reports and fails `check`.
- Test fake OSV answers unrecorded /v1/vulns IDs with 404 (as the real API does), so the existing spike goldens are unchanged.

